{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794552860,"nonce":"MoPDvEt3pzW7iEcrEHtIFKlxztM=","title":"A side module whose start function only calls helpers gets no thread-local block on its threads","message":"Reported by the hxn session (hxn issue 9ee1bd4, second failure): a generated Rust side module for wasm32-wasip1-threads aborts on the module's first spawned thread with \"fatal runtime error: current thread handle already set during thread spawn\", while a hand-written one with the same flags passes. hxn read it as a thread getting a copy of the main thread's live thread-locals.\n\nFindings, 2026-11-13, with hxn's own two built modules copied into a scratch directory and run with the release ash from 89001410 (both reproduce hxn's results):\n- That is not the mechanism. A probe that calls `thread::current()` on the main thread and then spawns gets a distinct, fresh handle in the child (current 1), and each spawned thread's thread-local sits at its own block (the main thread's at 9,909,968, children's at about 601 million, different each time).\n- What differs is whether the host finds the module's thread-local base. `ash_wasm_link::dylink::expose_tls_base` exports it so the loader can give every thread's instance a block (`give_thread_block` in ash_wasm_runtime/src/native/dylink.rs). It looks only at global.set instructions in the start function's own body, and accepts exactly one mutable i32 global.\n- The passing module's start function is `__wasm_init_memory`, which sets `__tls_base` (global 209): found.\n- The failing module's start function is `__wasm_start`, which sets nothing itself and calls `__wasm_apply_global_relocs` (sets global 490, `GOT.func.internal.\u003chashbrown ... reserve_rehash ...\u003e`, an internal GOT entry for a function whose address the module takes) and `__wasm_init_memory` (sets global 489, `__tls_base`). Nothing is found, `lib.tls` is None, no thread gets a block, and every thread's thread-locals sit at offset 0 in the program's low memory, shared with every other thread and with whatever else is there. Whether the abort fires depends on those bytes, which is why it looks order dependent.\n\nFix: find `__tls_base` without depending on the shape of the start function. In order of preference: the global named `__tls_base` in the name section when there is one; else the single mutable i32 global set by the functions the start function reaches that initialise passive data (those with memory.init or data.drop, i.e. `__wasm_init_memory`), not by `__wasm_apply_global_relocs`; else the current rule. Add a unit test with a start function that is a wrapper and a GOT global set beside the real one, and a run of hxn's generated fixture as the check.\n\nAlso look at the browser host: it has its own thread-local handling (side_module_threads.rs, browser_threads_start_their_thread_locals_from_the_initial_image) and may share the same assumption.","files":null}]}